  "chain": "fuel governor modification (Dec 9, 2025) inadvertently damages ES automatic-start wire [cause X] -> troubleshooting resources not fully engaged, delaying discovery -> 'A' EDG fails simulated ES automatic start test (Feb 2, 2026) -> 'A' EDG past inoperable ~56 days (exceeds 7-day TS AOT) -> concurrent 'A'/'B' EDG inoperability windows (loss-of-safety-function potential) [backup AACDG functional and available for manual start; FLEX equipment also available]",
  "golden_questions": [
    "Q1",
    "Q3",
    "Q4",
    "Q14"
  ],
  "notes": "Two distinct failure modes on 'A' EDG were conflated in early troubleshooting: (1) a defective start pilot relay causing remote MANUAL start failure (discovered Jan 18, resolved by relay replacement), and (2) an unrelated open-circuit wire in the AUTOMATIC (ES) start circuitry, introduced during the Dec 9 fuel governor modification, which was the actual TS-reportable past-inoperability condition. Modeled as separate FailureMode nodes per rule 5, both leading to the same past-inoperability Consequence. Cause code is 'X' (Other) per FORM-366 despite narrative discussing a contributing troubleshooting-resource-engagement cause; category kept as officially coded. AACDG explicitly stated functional/available during the event window, so modeled as BACKED_UP_BY; FLEX equipment mentioned only generically as an additional option and not given a dedicated System node. B EDG's own three short inoperability windows are described as contributing to the dual-inoperability consequence rather than separate failure chains, since no failure mode/cause was attributed to them beyond routine maintenance/testing."
}